+**Formulas:**
+* **Crossing Point Calculation** (for edge interpolation):
+  Given two endpoints $a, b$ with distances $d_a, d_b$ and positions $p_a, p_b$:
+  $$ \delta = d_b - d_a $$
+  $$ t = \begin{cases} 0.5 & \text{if } \delta = 0.0 \\ -\frac{d_a}{\delta} & \text{otherwise} \end{cases} $$
+  $$ p_{cross} = p_a (1 - t) + p_b t $$
+  For directed distance fields, the calculation is axis-aligned:
+  $$ \text{axis} = \text{argmax}_k |p_a[k] - p_b[k]| $$
+  $$ \delta = d_b[\text{axis}] - d_a[\text{axis}] $$
+  $$ t = \begin{cases} 0.5 & \text{if } \delta = 0.0 \\ -\frac{d_a[\text{axis}]}{\delta} & \text{otherwise} \end{cases} $$
+  $$ p_{cross} = p_a (1 - t) + p_b t $$ [S1]
+
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+* **Directed Distance Fields**: The implementation notes that constructing an octree over a directed distance field is not fully implemented [S1].
+* **Missing Nodes**: If a dual vertex is not found in the octree and its parent chain is exhausted without finding a value, the corner data remains incomplete [S1].
+* **Precision**: The use of floating-point distances and Morton coordinates requires careful handling of equality checks and boundary conditions, particularly when `delta` is zero [S1].
 
